COPY public.state_resources (id, state_fips, state_abbreviation, state_name, polling_place_url, voter_registration_url, vote_by_mail_info, polling_hours, id_requirements, sources) FROM stdin;
248b78b1-0901-41bf-980f-e843bd60914e	02	AK	Alaska	https://myvoterportal.alaska.gov	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Alaska is an all-mail-ballot option state. Any registered voter may request an absentee ballot without providing a reason. Absentee ballot requests must be received by the Division of Elections no later than 10 days before the election. Completed ballots must be postmarked by Election Day and received within 10 days after the election, or returned in person to a polling place or drop site by 8:00 PM on Election Day. Voters may also return ballots by mail, in-person delivery, or authorized representative.	In Alaska, polls are generally open from 7:00 AM to 8:00 PM local time on Election Day. However, because Alaska spans multiple time zones, specific opening and closing times may vary by region and precinct. Voters in line by 8:00 PM are permitted to cast their ballots.	Alaska requires voters to show identification at the polls. Acceptable forms of ID include a current and valid photo ID, a voter ID card issued by the State of Alaska, or a current utility bill, bank statement, paycheck, government check, or other government document showing the voter's name and address. Voters who do not have an acceptable ID may cast a questioned (provisional) ballot, which will be counted after their eligibility is verified.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://myvoterportal.alaska.gov", "source_name": "Alaska My Voter Portal"}, {"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "Find Your Polling Place | NASS"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://myvoterportal.alaska.gov", "source_name": "Vote.org"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://myvoterportal.alaska.gov", "source_name": "Alaska My Voter Portal"}, {"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "Find Your Polling Place | NASS"}, {"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "Find My Polling Place | U.S. Vote Foundation"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org absentee ballot"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "How to register to vote | USAGov"}]}
62de25ee-40d9-4e36-b4f9-6fd314fe4750	49	UT	Utah	https://votesearch.utah.gov/voter-search/search/search-by-address/how-and-where-can-i-vote	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Utah is an all-mail voting state. All active registered voters are automatically sent a mail ballot before each election. Voters may return their ballot by mail (must be postmarked by Election Day and received within 3 days after), dropped at an official ballot drop box, or delivered in person to a polling location or county election office by 8:00 PM on Election Day.	Utah polling locations are generally open from 7:00 AM to 8:00 PM on Election Day. Because Utah conducts elections primarily by mail, in-person vote centers serve as alternatives for voters who prefer to vote in person or need assistance. Hours may vary slightly by county.	Utah requires voters to show ID when voting in person. Acceptable forms of ID include a Utah driver license, state ID card, passport, military ID, or other government-issued photo ID. Voters who do not have a photo ID may use two forms of non-photo ID showing name and address (such as a utility bill or bank statement), or sign a poll book if a poll worker can verify their identity. First-time voters who registered by mail and did not provide ID verification during registration may be required to show ID.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://votesearch.utah.gov/voter-search/search/search-by-address/how-and-where-can-i-vote", "source_name": "Utah Voter Search – How and Where Can I Vote"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://votesearch.utah.gov/voter-search/search/search-by-address/how-and-where-can-i-vote", "source_name": "Vote.org"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://votesearch.utah.gov/voter-search/search/search-by-address/how-and-where-can-i-vote", "source_name": "Utah Voter Search – How and Where Can I Vote"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org – Absentee Ballot"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov – How to Register to Vote"}]}
fd721157-dc71-4179-b2c9-1c67e128df07	10	DE	Delaware	https://ivote.de.gov/VoterView	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Delaware voters may request an absentee ballot without providing a reason. Absentee ballot applications must be received by the Department of Elections no later than the Saturday before Election Day. Completed ballots must be received by 8:00 PM on Election Day; postmarks alone are not sufficient. Ballots may be returned by mail or dropped off in person at a county election office.	Delaware polling places are generally open from 7:00 AM to 8:00 PM on Election Day statewide.	Delaware does not require voters to show photo ID at the polls for most voters. First-time voters who registered by mail and did not provide verification at registration may be asked to show identification. Acceptable documents include a current and valid photo ID, a utility bill, bank statement, government check, paycheck, or other government document showing name and address.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS Can I Vote - Find Your Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation - Find My Polling Place"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://ivote.de.gov/VoterView", "source_name": "Delaware iVote Voter View"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org Absentee Ballot"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov - How to Register to Vote"}]}
cbec91b6-f0db-4b3a-8a2e-e8d6bb00813a	18	IN	Indiana	https://indianavoters.in.gov	https://indianavoters.in.gov	Indiana allows any registered voter to request an absentee ballot without needing an excuse. Voters must request an absentee ballot by mail no later than 11 days before the election. Completed mail ballots must be received by the county election board by noon on Election Day. Ballots may be returned by mail or delivered in person to the county election board.	Indiana polling places are generally open from 6:00 AM to 6:00 PM local time on Election Day. However, voters in line by 6:00 PM are permitted to cast their ballot. Hours may vary slightly by county, so voters should confirm with their local county election office.	Indiana requires voters to present a photo ID at the polls. Acceptable forms of ID include an Indiana driver's license, state-issued ID card, U.S. passport, or military ID. The ID must display the voter's name, photo, and an expiration date showing the ID is current or expired after the last general election. Voters without an acceptable photo ID may cast a provisional ballot and must provide proof of identification to the county election board by noon 10 days after the election for the ballot to be counted.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://indianavoters.in.gov", "source_name": "Indiana Voters – Official Indiana Election Portal"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://indianavoters.in.gov", "source_name": "Vote.org"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://indianavoters.in.gov", "source_name": "Indiana Voters – Official Indiana Election Portal"}, {"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "Find Your Polling Place | NASS"}, {"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "Find My Polling Place | U.S. Vote Foundation"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org Absentee Ballot"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://indianavoters.in.gov", "source_name": "Indiana Voters – Official Indiana Election Portal"}, {"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "How to register to vote | USAGov"}]}
6d2af38b-5f69-4865-8b8b-4c350b26d908	23	ME	Maine	https://www.maine.gov/portal/government/edemocracy/voter_lookup.php	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Maine allows any registered voter to request an absentee ballot without needing an excuse. Absentee ballot requests can be made up to 3 business days before Election Day. Completed ballots must be received by the municipal clerk by 8:00 PM on Election Day; postmarks alone are not sufficient — the ballot must be physically received by the deadline. Voters may return absentee ballots by mail, in person to their municipal clerk, or via a designated drop box where available.	In Maine, polls are generally open from 6:00 AM to 8:00 PM on Election Day, though some smaller municipalities may open as late as 10:00 AM. Exact hours can vary by municipality, so voters should confirm with their local town or city clerk.	Maine does not require voters to show photo ID at the polls. First-time voters who registered by mail and did not provide verification at registration may be asked to show identification (such as a utility bill, bank statement, or government document showing name and address), but a photo ID is not required. Most voters simply state their name and address to a poll worker.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS – Find Your Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation – Find My Polling Place"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.maine.gov/portal/government/edemocracy/voter_lookup.php", "source_name": "Maine Voter Lookup – Maine.gov"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org – Absentee Ballot"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "How to register to vote | USAGov"}]}
52136466-6afe-48a7-a4a2-b07160b18944	25	MA	Massachusetts	https://www.sec.state.ma.us/wheredoivotema/bal/MyElectionInfo.aspx	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Massachusetts allows any registered voter to vote by mail (absentee ballot) without needing an excuse. Voters must request an absentee ballot from their local election office. For a primary or general election, applications must typically be received by the close of business on the day before the election. Completed ballots must be returned to the local election office by 8:00 PM on Election Day, either by mail or hand delivery. Ballots returned by mail must be received (not just postmarked) by Election Day closing time to be counted.	In Massachusetts, polls are generally open from 7:00 AM to 8:00 PM on Election Day statewide, though some municipalities may open as early as 6:00 AM. Any voter in line by 8:00 PM is entitled to cast a ballot.	Massachusetts does not require voters to show photo ID at the polls in most cases. First-time voters who registered by mail and did not provide verification at registration may be asked to show identification (such as a utility bill, bank statement, government check, or other document showing name and address). Established voters who are already on the rolls are not required to present ID.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS – Find Your Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.sec.state.ma.us/wheredoivotema/bal/MyElectionInfo.aspx", "source_name": "Vote.org"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.sec.state.ma.us/wheredoivotema/bal/MyElectionInfo.aspx", "source_name": "Massachusetts Secretary of State – Where Do I Vote"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org – Absentee Ballot"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov – How to Register to Vote"}]}
6193e36d-93a0-4e0d-9c55-fe80d1baefa6	30	MT	Montana	https://app.mt.gov/voterinfo	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Montana is an all-mail ballot state. All active registered voters are automatically sent a mail ballot before each election. Voters may also choose to vote in person at a polling place or drop box. Mail ballots must be received by 8:00 PM on Election Day, or postmarked by Election Day and received within 3 days after the election. Voters can return ballots by mail, at a county election office, or at an official drop box.	Montana polling places for in-person voting are generally open from 7:00 AM to 8:00 PM local time on Election Day. Any voter in line by 8:00 PM is entitled to vote. Specific hours may vary slightly by county; voters should confirm with their local county election office.	Montana requires voters to present valid photo ID or another acceptable form of identification when voting in person. Acceptable photo IDs include a Montana driver's license, state ID card, U.S. passport, military ID, or tribal photo ID. Voters without photo ID may present a current utility bill, bank statement, paycheck, or government document showing name and address, or may sign a poll book if a poll worker can verify their identity. First-time voters who registered by mail and did not provide ID verification at registration may face additional requirements.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://app.mt.gov/voterinfo", "source_name": "Montana Voter Information Portal"}, {"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS - Find Your Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://app.mt.gov/voterinfo", "source_name": "Vote.org"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://app.mt.gov/voterinfo", "source_name": "Montana Voter Information Portal"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org - Absentee Ballot Information"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov - How to Register to Vote"}]}
2f15de7b-9e4b-4dc5-93c8-8b35e027563b	01	AL	Alabama	https://myinfo.alabamavotes.gov/voterview	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Alabama voters may request an absentee ballot without needing to provide a specific excuse only under certain qualifying circumstances (e.g., illness, absence from county on Election Day, or physical disability). Absentee ballot applications must generally be received by the county absentee election manager no later than 5 days before the election. Completed absentee ballots must be received by the county absentee election manager by the close of polls on Election Day; postmarking alone is not sufficient. Ballots may be returned by mail or delivered in person to the county absentee election manager's office.	Alabama polling places are generally open from 7:00 AM to 7:00 PM local time on Election Day. Voters who are in line by 7:00 PM are entitled to cast their ballot.	Alabama requires voters to present a valid photo ID at the polls. Acceptable forms of photo ID include an Alabama driver's license, a non-driver Alabama ID card, a U.S. passport, a government employee ID, a military ID, a tribal ID, or another government-issued photo ID. Voters who do not have a photo ID may cast a provisional ballot and must verify their identity within a specified period after Election Day for the ballot to be counted.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS - Find Your Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation - Find My Polling Place"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://myinfo.alabamavotes.gov/voterview", "source_name": "Alabama Votes VoterView"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org - Absentee Ballot"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov - How to Register to Vote"}]}
7670635e-44b9-4325-a327-7813f7df4279	04	AZ	Arizona	https://my.arizona.vote/WhereToVote.aspx?s=address	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Arizona is an all-mail ballot state for most voters. Active early voters receive a ballot automatically before each election. Voters who are not on the Permanent Early Voting List (PEVL) may request an early ballot no later than 11 days before the election. Mail ballots must be received by election officials by 7:00 PM on Election Day to be counted; postmark alone is not sufficient. Voters may also return their mail ballot in person at any official drop box or voting location by 7:00 PM on Election Day.	Arizona polling locations are generally open from 6:00 AM to 7:00 PM local time on Election Day. Any voter in line by 7:00 PM is entitled to cast a ballot.	Arizona requires voters to show photo ID at the polls. Acceptable photo IDs include an Arizona driver's license, state-issued ID, tribal enrollment card with photo, or other government-issued photo ID showing name and address. Voters without photo ID may present two non-photo documents showing name and address (e.g., utility bill, bank statement, government document). Voters who cannot provide ID may cast a provisional ballot and must verify their identity with the county recorder within five business days.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://my.arizona.vote/WhereToVote.aspx?s=address", "source_name": "Arizona Voter Information Portal"}, {"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS - Find Your Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://my.arizona.vote/WhereToVote.aspx?s=address", "source_name": "Vote.org"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://my.arizona.vote/WhereToVote.aspx?s=address", "source_name": "Arizona Voter Information Portal"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org - Absentee Ballot"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov - How to Register to Vote"}]}
836d9c59-b2d4-4710-8241-3e8628a4481c	09	CT	Connecticut	https://portaldir.ct.gov/sots/LookUp.aspx	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Connecticut allows any registered voter to vote by absentee ballot without needing an excuse. Voters must request an absentee ballot from their town clerk. For a primary or general election, absentee ballot applications must typically be received by the town clerk by the day before the election. Completed absentee ballots must be returned to the town clerk and received by 8:00 PM on Election Day; ballots may be returned by mail or delivered in person to the town clerk's office or an official drop box.	Connecticut polling places are generally open from 6:00 AM to 8:00 PM on Election Day statewide. Any voter who is in line by 8:00 PM is entitled to vote.	Connecticut does not require voters to show photo ID at the polls in most cases. First-time voters who registered by mail and did not provide verification at registration may be asked to show identification (such as a utility bill, bank statement, or other document showing name and address). Voters who cannot provide ID may cast a provisional ballot.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "Find Your Polling Place | NASS"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://portaldir.ct.gov/sots/LookUp.aspx", "source_name": "Vote.org"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://portaldir.ct.gov/sots/LookUp.aspx", "source_name": "Connecticut Secretary of the State Polling Place Locator (via vote.org)"}, {"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "Find Your Polling Place | NASS"}, {"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "Find My Polling Place | U.S. Vote Foundation"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org Absentee Ballot Information"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "How to register to vote | USAGov"}]}
1bd05c34-de7e-4bfe-8d26-53a61ef06b39	46	SD	South Dakota	https://www.vote.org/polling-place-locator	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	South Dakota allows any registered voter to request an absentee ballot without needing an excuse. Absentee ballot applications must be received by the county auditor by 5:00 PM on the day before the election. Completed absentee ballots must be received by the county auditor by the close of polls (7:00 PM) on Election Day — ballots postmarked by Election Day but received after that deadline are not counted. Voters may return absentee ballots by mail or in person to their county auditor's office.	South Dakota polls are generally open from 7:00 AM to 7:00 PM local time on Election Day. Voters in line by 7:00 PM are permitted to cast their ballot.	South Dakota requires voters to present photo ID at the polls. Acceptable forms of photo ID include a South Dakota driver's license, state ID card, passport, tribal ID with photo, or other government-issued photo ID. Voters who do not have an acceptable photo ID may cast a provisional ballot and must provide proof of identity to the county auditor before canvassing is complete for the ballot to be counted.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS - Find Your Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation - Find My Polling Place"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/polling-place-locator", "source_name": "vote.org"}, {"source_url": "https://vip.sdsos.gov/VIPLogin.aspx", "source_name": "South Dakota Secretary of State Voter Information Portal"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org - Absentee Ballot"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov - How to Register to Vote"}]}
ceab58d9-4a7d-4fe1-8cfb-ee2969fca2c1	56	WY	Wyoming	https://soswy.state.wy.us/Elections/PollPlace/Default	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Wyoming allows any registered voter to request an absentee ballot without needing to provide a reason. Absentee ballot requests must be submitted to the county clerk no later than the day before the election. Completed ballots must be received by the county clerk by the close of polls on Election Day; postmarks alone are not sufficient. Voters may return ballots by mail or in person to their county clerk's office.	Polling hours in Wyoming are generally 7:00 AM to 7:00 PM local time on Election Day. Voters in line at 7:00 PM are permitted to cast their ballots. Specific hours may vary slightly by county, so voters should confirm with their local county clerk.	Wyoming requires voters to show identification at the polls. Acceptable forms of ID include a Wyoming driver's license, a U.S. passport, a military ID, a tribal ID, or another government-issued photo ID. Voters who do not have an acceptable photo ID may cast a provisional ballot and must provide proof of identity to the county clerk within three days after the election for the ballot to be counted.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://sos.wyo.gov/Elections/PollPlace/Default.aspx", "source_name": "Wyoming Secretary of State – Polling Place Locator"}, {"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS – Find Your Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation – Voter ID Laws"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://soswy.state.wy.us/Elections/PollPlace/Default", "source_name": "soswy.state.wy.us"}, {"source_url": "https://sos.wyo.gov/Elections/PollPlace/Default.aspx", "source_name": "Wyoming Secretary of State – Polling Place Locator"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org – Absentee Ballot Information"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov – How to Register to Vote"}]}
64a5d54a-61a2-4dfd-b3b8-333f966c78dc	05	AR	Arkansas	https://www.vote.org/polling-place-locator	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Arkansas allows any registered voter to vote absentee (by mail) without needing an excuse. Voters must request an absentee ballot from their county clerk. The request deadline is generally 7 days before the election. Completed ballots must be received by the county clerk by 7:30 PM on Election Day, or postmarked by Election Day and received within 5 days after the election. Ballots may be returned by mail or delivered in person to the county clerk's office.	Arkansas polls are generally open from 7:30 AM to 7:30 PM local time on Election Day. Voters in line by 7:30 PM are permitted to cast their ballot.	Arkansas requires voters to present photo ID at the polls. Acceptable forms include a driver's license, U.S. passport, employee ID, student ID, military ID, or other government-issued photo ID. Voters who do not present a valid photo ID may cast a provisional ballot, which will be counted if the voter's identity is verified.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.voterview.ar-nova.org/voterview", "source_name": "VoteView Arkansas Polling Place Locator"}, {"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS – Find Your Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.voterview.ar-nova.org/voterview", "source_name": "Vote.org"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/polling-place-locator", "source_name": "vote.org"}, {"source_url": "https://www.voterview.ar-nova.org/voterview", "source_name": "VoteView Arkansas Polling Place Locator"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org – Absentee Ballot Information"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov – How to Register to Vote"}]}
70d373c6-58f9-4323-9f3c-69f3de646613	11	DC	District of Columbia	https://www.vote.org/polling-place-locator	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	DC residents may vote absentee (by mail) without needing an excuse. Voters can request an absentee ballot online, by mail, or in person through the DC Board of Elections. Absentee ballot requests must generally be submitted at least 7 days before Election Day. Completed ballots must be received by the DC Board of Elections by 8:00 PM on Election Day if returned by mail, or may be dropped off at any vote center by closing time on Election Day. DC also automatically mails ballots to all registered voters for certain elections.	In the District of Columbia, vote centers are open from 7:00 AM to 8:00 PM on Election Day. Any voter in line by 8:00 PM is entitled to cast a ballot.	DC does not require voters to show photo ID at the polls in most cases. First-time voters who did not provide verification when registering may be asked to show ID. Acceptable forms include a government-issued photo ID, a utility bill, bank statement, or other document showing the voter's name and address. Voters who cannot provide ID may cast a provisional ballot.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://dcboe.org/voters/find-out-where-to-vote/vote-center-locator-tool", "source_name": "DC Board of Elections Vote Center Locator"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation - Find My Polling Place"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/polling-place-locator", "source_name": "vote.org"}, {"source_url": "https://dcboe.org/voters/find-out-where-to-vote/vote-center-locator-tool", "source_name": "DC Board of Elections Vote Center Locator"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org Absentee Ballot"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov - How to Register to Vote"}]}
01311e31-1c31-4f06-893f-3a1f5f516811	13	GA	Georgia	https://www.mvp.sos.ga.gov/MVP/mvp.do	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Georgia voters may request an absentee ballot without providing a reason. Absentee ballot applications must be received by the county election office no later than 11 days before Election Day. Completed absentee ballots must be received by the county election office by 7:00 PM on Election Day; postmarks do not count. Ballots may be returned by mail, in person to the county election office, or deposited in an official absentee ballot drop box located at county election offices and early voting sites.	Georgia polling places are open from 7:00 AM to 7:00 PM on Election Day. Any voter in line by 7:00 PM is entitled to cast a ballot. Hours may vary slightly by county; voters should confirm with their local county election office.	Georgia requires photo ID to vote in person. Acceptable forms include a Georgia driver's license, state ID card, passport, military ID, or tribal ID. Voters who do not have an acceptable photo ID may cast a provisional ballot and must present ID to their county election office within three days after the election for the ballot to be counted. Voters casting absentee ballots by mail must provide their driver's license number or state ID number, or include a copy of acceptable photo ID with their ballot.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS - Find Your Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.mvp.sos.ga.gov/MVP/mvp.do", "source_name": "Vote.org"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.mvp.sos.ga.gov/MVP/mvp.do", "source_name": "Georgia Secretary of State - My Voter Page"}, {"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation - Find My Polling Place"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org - Absentee Ballot"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov - How to Register to Vote"}]}
dddf8517-7720-4b35-8d46-ca321cb34d5f	17	IL	Illinois	https://ova.elections.il.gov/PollingPlaceLookup.aspx	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Illinois voters may vote by mail (absentee) without needing an excuse. Voters must request a mail-in ballot no later than 5 days before the election. Completed ballots must be received by the county clerk by 7:00 PM on Election Day if returned in person, or postmarked by Election Day and received within 14 days after the election if returned by mail. Ballots may be returned by mail or delivered in person to the county clerk's office or an official drop box.	Illinois polling places are generally open from 6:00 AM to 7:00 PM on Election Day. Any voter in line by 7:00 PM is entitled to vote. Early voting hours may vary by county.	Illinois does not require voters to show photo ID at the polls in most cases. First-time voters who registered by mail and did not provide verification at registration may be asked to show identification (such as a utility bill, bank statement, government check, paycheck, or other government document showing name and address). Photo ID is not required for the vast majority of Illinois voters.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "Find Your Polling Place | NASS"}, {"source_url": "https://ova.elections.il.gov/PollingPlaceLookup.aspx", "source_name": "Illinois State Board of Elections Polling Place Lookup"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://ova.elections.il.gov/PollingPlaceLookup.aspx", "source_name": "Vote.org"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://ova.elections.il.gov/PollingPlaceLookup.aspx", "source_name": "Illinois State Board of Elections Polling Place Lookup"}, {"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "Find Your Polling Place | NASS"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org absentee ballot"}, {"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "Find My Polling Place | U.S. Vote Foundation"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "How to register to vote | USAGov"}]}
7a8b1e3c-46a1-4898-93e2-61997b0369d7	20	KS	Kansas	https://www.vote.org/polling-place-locator	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Kansas voters may vote by mail (advance/absentee ballot) without needing an excuse. Voters must request an advance ballot by mail no later than the Tuesday before the election (7 days before Election Day). Completed ballots must be received by the county election office by the close of polls on Election Day, or postmarked by Election Day and received within 3 days after the election. Ballots may be returned by mail or delivered in person to the county election office.	Kansas polling places are generally open from 7:00 AM to 7:00 PM local time on Election Day. Any voter in line by 7:00 PM is entitled to vote. Hours may vary slightly by county, so voters should confirm with their local county election office.	Kansas requires voters to show photo ID at the polls. Acceptable forms of photo ID include a Kansas driver's license, state-issued ID, U.S. passport, military ID, or other government-issued photo ID. Voters who do not have an acceptable photo ID may cast a provisional ballot, but must provide ID to the county election office before the canvass deadline for the ballot to be counted.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "Find Your Polling Place | NASS"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://myvoteinfo.voteks.org/VoterView", "source_name": "Vote.org"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/polling-place-locator", "source_name": "vote.org"}, {"source_url": "https://myvoteinfo.voteks.org/VoterView", "source_name": "VoterView – Kansas Polling Place Locator"}, {"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "Find Your Polling Place | NASS"}, {"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "Find My Polling Place | U.S. Vote Foundation"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org – Absentee Ballot"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "How to register to vote | USAGov"}]}
db6b299a-a351-4127-ae68-5bc2428460fc	22	LA	Louisiana	https://voterportal.sos.la.gov	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Louisiana allows any registered voter to vote absentee by mail. Voters must submit an absentee ballot application to their parish registrar of voters. Applications must be received no later than 4:30 PM on the 4th day before the election. Completed ballots must be received by the registrar by 4:30 PM on Election Day; postmarks alone are not sufficient. Ballots may be returned by mail or delivered in person to the registrar's office.	Louisiana polling places are generally open from 6:00 AM to 8:00 PM local time on Election Day. Any voter in line by 8:00 PM is entitled to cast a ballot.	Louisiana requires voters to present photo ID at the polls. Acceptable forms include a Louisiana driver's license, Louisiana special ID card, U.S. passport, military ID, or other government-issued photo ID. Voters who do not have an acceptable photo ID may cast a provisional ballot, which will be counted if their identity is confirmed by election officials.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://voterportal.sos.la.gov", "source_name": "Louisiana Voter Portal – Secretary of State"}, {"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS – Find Your Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://voterportal.sos.la.gov", "source_name": "Vote.org"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://voterportal.sos.la.gov", "source_name": "Louisiana Voter Portal – Secretary of State"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org – Absentee Ballot"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov – How to Register to Vote"}]}
e55dddf2-3fc1-4e34-9aa1-8ab8cfca34fa	27	MN	Minnesota	https://pollfinder.sos.state.mn.us	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Minnesota allows any registered voter to request an absentee ballot without providing a reason. Absentee ballot applications must be received by your county auditor no later than the Friday before the election. Completed ballots must be received by election day (not just postmarked); voters may return ballots by mail, in person to their county auditor or municipal clerk, or at a designated drop-off location. Voters may also vote in person at an early absentee voting location starting 46 days before the election.	In Minnesota, polls are open statewide from 7:00 AM to 8:00 PM on Election Day. Any voter in line by 8:00 PM is entitled to vote.	Minnesota does not require a photo ID to vote. Voters must provide their name, address, and date of birth. Registered voters may show a valid photo ID, a voter registration card, or other documentation showing name and address. First-time voters who registered by mail and have not previously voted in a federal election must show ID or proof of residence. Minnesota also allows vouching, where a registered voter in the same precinct can vouch for a voter who cannot provide required documentation.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS - Find Your Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation - Find My Polling Place"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://pollfinder.sos.state.mn.us", "source_name": "Minnesota Secretary of State Poll Finder"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org - Absentee Ballot"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov - How to Register to Vote"}]}
74f4ca16-c298-40df-aa6d-282a83140ccf	29	MO	Missouri	https://voteroutreach.sos.mo.gov/PRD/VoterOutreach/VOSearch.aspx	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Missouri voters may request an absentee ballot without needing to provide a reason. Absentee ballot applications must be received by the local election authority no later than the second Wednesday before the election. Completed absentee ballots must be received by the local election authority by the close of polls on Election Day — Missouri does not count ballots that arrive after Election Day, even if postmarked on time. Ballots may be returned by mail or delivered in person to the local election authority.	In Missouri, polls are generally open from 6:00 AM to 7:00 PM local time on Election Day. Any voter who is in line by 7:00 PM is entitled to cast a ballot.	Missouri requires voters to present photo ID at the polls. Acceptable forms include a Missouri driver's license or non-driver ID, U.S. passport, military ID, or other government-issued photo ID. Voters who do not have a qualifying photo ID may cast a provisional ballot and must return to the election authority with acceptable ID by the close of business on the Friday following the election for the ballot to be counted. Missouri offers a free non-driver photo ID for voting purposes to eligible residents.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS – Find Your Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation – Voter ID Laws"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://voteroutreach.sos.mo.gov/PRD/VoterOutreach/VOSearch.aspx", "source_name": "Missouri Secretary of State – Voter Outreach Polling Place Search"}, {"source_url": "https://www.sos.mo.gov/elections/pollingplacelookup", "source_name": "Missouri Secretary of State – Polling Place Lookup"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "Vote.org – Absentee Ballot Information"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov – How to Register to Vote"}]}
306b1d0f-6906-4cfa-975a-3aec72a3bf56	32	NV	Nevada	https://www.vote.org/polling-place-locator	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Nevada is an all-mail ballot state. All active registered voters are automatically sent a mail ballot before each election. Voters may return their mail ballot by mail (must be postmarked on or before Election Day and received within 7 days after Election Day), at an official drop box, or in person at any early voting site or Election Day voting location. Voters who prefer to vote in person may do so at any vote center in their county.	Nevada does not have a single statewide polling hour mandate for all counties; hours vary by county. However, most vote centers and Election Day polling locations are generally open from 7:00 AM to 7:00 PM local time. Early voting periods typically span several weeks before Election Day, with hours set by each county.	Nevada does not require voters to show photo ID at the polls in most circumstances. First-time voters who registered by mail and did not provide verification may be asked to show ID. Acceptable forms include a driver's license, utility bill, bank statement, government check, paycheck, or other government document showing name and address. Voters without ID may cast a provisional ballot.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.nvsos.gov/votersearch", "source_name": "Nevada Secretary of State Voter Search"}, {"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS - Find Your Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation - Find My Polling Place"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/polling-place-locator", "source_name": "vote.org"}, {"source_url": "https://www.nvsos.gov/votersearch", "source_name": "Nevada Secretary of State Voter Search"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org - Absentee Ballot Information"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov - How to Register to Vote"}]}
262eb1c5-61ec-4337-9e13-773c5dd6668e	35	NM	New Mexico	https://voterportal.servis.sos.state.nm.us/WhereToVoteAddress.aspx	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	New Mexico is an all-mail ballot state. All registered voters are automatically sent a mail ballot before each election. Voters may return their ballot by mail (must be postmarked by Election Day and received within 3 days after Election Day), dropped at an official drop box, or delivered in person to a county clerk's office or early voting site. Voters may also choose to vote in person at a polling place instead.	In New Mexico, polls are open from 7:00 AM to 7:00 PM on Election Day. Any voter in line by 7:00 PM is entitled to vote.	New Mexico does not require voters to show photo ID at the polls. Instead, voters provide their name, address, and year of birth, which are verified against the voter rolls. First-time voters who registered by mail and did not provide ID during registration may be asked to show ID.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS - Find Your Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://voterportal.servis.sos.state.nm.us/WhereToVoteAddress.aspx", "source_name": "Vote.org"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://voterportal.servis.sos.state.nm.us/WhereToVoteAddress.aspx", "source_name": "New Mexico Secretary of State Voter Portal"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org - Absentee Ballot Information"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov - How to Register to Vote"}]}
495a17e4-9bf9-4a79-aa8d-2a11525c4882	37	NC	North Carolina	https://www.vote.org/polling-place-locator	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	North Carolina voters may request an absentee ballot without providing a reason. Absentee ballot requests must be received by the county board of elections by 5:00 PM on the Tuesday before Election Day. Completed ballots must be received by county boards by 7:30 PM on Election Day, or postmarked by Election Day and received within three days after Election Day. Ballots may be returned by mail or delivered in person to the county board of elections.	North Carolina polling places are generally open from 6:30 AM to 7:30 PM on Election Day. Any voter in line by 7:30 PM is entitled to vote. Early voting hours vary by county and site.	North Carolina requires voters to show photo ID at the polls. Acceptable forms include a North Carolina driver's license, U.S. passport, military ID, or other government-issued photo ID. Voters who do not have an acceptable photo ID may cast a provisional ballot and have until the end of the canvass period to provide ID or complete an ID exception form based on a reasonable impediment.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS Can I Vote - Find Your Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation - Find My Polling Place"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/polling-place-locator", "source_name": "vote.org"}, {"source_url": "https://vt.ncsbe.gov/PPLkup", "source_name": "North Carolina State Board of Elections Polling Place Lookup"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org Absentee Ballot Information"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov - How to Register to Vote"}]}
3d85856f-6778-4b10-b977-826c37700c24	39	OH	Ohio	https://voterlookup.ohiosos.gov/VoterLookup.aspx	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Ohio voters may request an absentee ballot without providing a reason. Absentee ballot applications must be received by the county board of elections no later than 3 days before Election Day. Completed ballots must be received by the board of elections by the close of polls on Election Day, or postmarked by the day before Election Day and received within 10 days after the election. Ballots may be returned by mail or delivered in person to the county board of elections.	Ohio polling places are open from 6:30 AM to 7:30 PM local time on Election Day. Any voter in line by 7:30 PM is entitled to vote.	Ohio requires voters to present identification at the polls. Acceptable forms of ID include an Ohio driver's license or state ID, a military ID, a U.S. passport, or a document showing the voter's name and current address (such as a utility bill, bank statement, government check, or paycheck). Voters who do not have an acceptable photo ID may cast a provisional ballot and provide the last four digits of their Social Security number, or return within 7 days with acceptable ID to have the ballot counted.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.sos.state.oh.us/elections/voters/toolkit/polling-location", "source_name": "Ohio Secretary of State Polling Location Toolkit"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation - Find My Polling Place"}, {"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS Can I Vote"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://voterlookup.ohiosos.gov/VoterLookup.aspx", "source_name": "Ohio Secretary of State Voter Lookup"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org Absentee Ballot"}, {"source_url": "https://www.sos.state.oh.us/elections/voters/toolkit/polling-location", "source_name": "Ohio Secretary of State Polling Location Toolkit"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov - How to Register to Vote"}]}
572ee4dc-f53a-49ef-a366-5d64c23e57a5	40	OK	Oklahoma	https://www.vote.org/polling-place-locator	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Oklahoma voters may request an absentee ballot without providing a reason. Absentee ballot applications must be received by the county election board no later than 5:00 PM on the Wednesday before the election. Completed ballots must be received by the county election board by 7:00 PM on Election Day; postmarks alone are not sufficient. Voters may return ballots by mail or in person to their county election board.	Oklahoma polling places are generally open from 7:00 AM to 7:00 PM local time on Election Day. Voters in line by 7:00 PM are permitted to cast their ballot.	Oklahoma requires voters to present an approved photo ID at the polls. Acceptable forms include an Oklahoma driver's license, state ID card, U.S. passport, military ID, or other government-issued photo ID. Voters who do not have an acceptable photo ID may cast a provisional ballot and must provide proof of identity to the county election board by the Friday following the election for the ballot to be counted.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://okvoterportal.okelections.us", "source_name": "Oklahoma Voter Portal – OKElections.us"}, {"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS – Find Your Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation – Find My Polling Place"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/polling-place-locator", "source_name": "vote.org"}, {"source_url": "https://okvoterportal.okelections.us", "source_name": "Oklahoma Voter Portal – OKElections.us"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "Vote.org – Absentee Ballot"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov – How to Register to Vote"}]}
e38c4af1-c5ff-46ac-9065-f1aeb1019e1e	42	PA	Pennsylvania	https://www.pavoterservices.pa.gov/Pages/PollingPlaceInfo.aspx	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Pennsylvania voters may vote by mail-in or absentee ballot without needing to provide a reason. Voters must apply for a mail-in ballot by 5:00 PM on the Tuesday before the election (7 days before Election Day). Completed ballots must be received by the county election board by 8:00 PM on Election Day — postmarks do not count. Ballots may be returned by mail or dropped off in person at the county election office or an official drop box.	In Pennsylvania, polls are open statewide from 7:00 AM to 8:00 PM on Election Day. Any voter who is in line by 8:00 PM is entitled to vote.	Pennsylvania requires ID only for first-time voters who did not provide a verifiable ID number when registering. First-time voters must show an acceptable photo or non-photo ID (such as a driver's license, passport, utility bill, bank statement, or government document showing name and address). Voters who have voted at their polling place before are not required to show ID and instead sign a poll book to confirm their identity. Voters without ID may cast a provisional ballot.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS – Find Your Polling Place"}, {"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation – Find My Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.pavoterservices.pa.gov/Pages/PollingPlaceInfo.aspx", "source_name": "Vote.org"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.pavoterservices.pa.gov/Pages/PollingPlaceInfo.aspx", "source_name": "Pennsylvania Voter Services – Polling Place Info"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org – Absentee Ballot"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov – How to Register to Vote"}]}
65911363-e0e4-430f-81d8-1b55114ffc24	44	RI	Rhode Island	https://vote.sos.ri.gov/Home/PollingPlaces?ActiveFlag=2	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Rhode Island allows any registered voter to vote by mail (absentee ballot) without needing an excuse. Voters must request a mail ballot from their local board of canvassers. For a primary or general election, mail ballot applications must generally be received by the board of canvassers no later than 21 days before the election. Completed ballots must be received by the local board of canvassers by 8:00 PM on Election Day; postmarks alone are not sufficient — the ballot must be physically received by the deadline. Ballots may be returned by mail or delivered in person to the local board of canvassers.	Rhode Island polling places are open statewide from 7:00 AM to 8:00 PM on Election Day. Any voter who is in line by 8:00 PM is entitled to cast a ballot.	Rhode Island does not require voters to show photo ID at the polls in most cases. First-time voters who registered by mail and did not provide verification at registration may be asked to show identification. Acceptable documents include a copy of a current utility bill, bank statement, government check, paycheck, or other government document showing the voter's name and address. Voters who cannot provide ID may cast a provisional ballot.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://vote.sos.ri.gov/Home/PollingPlaces", "source_name": "Rhode Island Secretary of State – Polling Places"}, {"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS – Find Your Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation – Find My Polling Place"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://vote.sos.ri.gov/Home/PollingPlaces?ActiveFlag=2", "source_name": "Rhode Island Secretary of State – Polling Places"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org – Absentee Ballot"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov – How to Register to Vote"}]}
409c60d2-6e51-40aa-8fe7-043fe30cfcf8	47	TN	Tennessee	https://web.go-vote-tn.elections.tn.gov	https://tnmap.tn.gov/voterlookup	Tennessee allows any registered voter to vote absentee by mail. Voters must submit an absentee ballot application to their county election commission. Applications must be received at least 7 days before the election. Completed ballots must be received by the county election commission by the close of polls on Election Day; postmarks alone are not sufficient. Voters may return ballots by mail or in person to their county election commission office.	Tennessee polling places are generally open from 7:00 AM to 8:00 PM local time on Election Day. Voters in line at 8:00 PM are permitted to cast their ballots. Specific hours may vary slightly by county, so voters should confirm with their local county election commission.	Tennessee requires voters to present photo ID at the polls. Acceptable forms of photo ID include a Tennessee driver's license, U.S. passport, photo ID issued by Tennessee or the federal government, U.S. military photo ID, or a Tennessee handgun carry permit with a photo. Voters who do not have an acceptable photo ID may cast a provisional ballot and must provide valid photo ID to their county election commission within two business days after the election for the ballot to be counted.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS – Find Your Polling Place"}, {"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation – Find My Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://web.go-vote-tn.elections.tn.gov", "source_name": "Vote.org"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://web.go-vote-tn.elections.tn.gov", "source_name": "Tennessee Secretary of State – Go Vote TN Polling Place Locator"}, {"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS – Find Your Polling Place"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org – Absentee Ballot Information"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://tnmap.tn.gov/voterlookup", "source_name": "Tennessee Voter Registration Information Lookup – Tennessee Secretary of State"}, {"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov – How to Register to Vote"}]}
f2bc0eee-b248-4bfb-9eda-a1b9da93f5d3	50	VT	Vermont	https://sos.vermont.gov/elections/voters/polling-places	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Vermont allows any registered voter to vote by absentee (mail-in) ballot without needing an excuse. Voters can request an absentee ballot online, by mail, or in person from their town clerk. For general elections, absentee ballot requests must typically be received by the Friday before Election Day. Completed ballots must be returned to the town clerk by 7:00 PM on Election Day, either by mail (received by close of polls), in person, or via an authorized messenger.	Vermont polling places are generally open from 7:00 AM to 7:00 PM on Election Day, though some smaller towns may open as early as 5:00 AM or have slightly different hours. Hours can vary by town, so voters should confirm with their local town clerk.	Vermont does not require voters to show photo ID at the polls. First-time voters who registered by mail and did not provide verification may be asked to show identification, but in general, voters state their name and address to a poll worker and sign the checklist. No photo ID is required for most voters.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://sos.vermont.gov/elections/voters/polling-places", "source_name": "Vermont Secretary of State – Polling Places"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation – Find My Polling Place"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://sos.vermont.gov/elections/voters/polling-places", "source_name": "Vermont Secretary of State – Polling Places"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org – Absentee Ballot"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov – How to Register to Vote"}]}
ce17e61f-7c70-4f95-91de-0f38c181d541	51	VA	Virginia	https://www.elections.virginia.gov/citizen-portal/index.html	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Virginia allows any registered voter to request an absentee ballot without needing an excuse. Absentee ballot applications must be received by the registrar no later than 5 p.m. on the Friday before the election. Completed ballots must be received by the local electoral board by 7 p.m. on Election Day; postmarks do not count — the ballot must be physically received by the deadline. Voters may return ballots by mail, in person to the registrar's office, or to a designated drop-off location.	Virginia polling places are open from 6:00 a.m. to 7:00 p.m. on Election Day statewide. Any voter who is in line by 7:00 p.m. is entitled to vote.	Virginia requires voters to show an acceptable photo ID at the polls. Acceptable forms include a Virginia driver's license, U.S. passport, employer-issued photo ID, student photo ID from a Virginia college or university, or other government-issued photo ID. Voters who do not have an acceptable photo ID may cast a provisional ballot and must provide a copy of acceptable ID to their local electoral board by noon on the Friday following the election for the ballot to be counted.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.elections.virginia.gov/citizen-portal/index.html", "source_name": "Virginia Department of Elections Citizen Portal"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation – Find My Polling Place"}, {"source_url": "https://www.elections.virginia.gov/citizen-portal/index.html", "source_name": "Virginia Department of Elections Citizen Portal"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.elections.virginia.gov/citizen-portal/index.html", "source_name": "Virginia Department of Elections Citizen Portal"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org – Absentee Ballot"}, {"source_url": "https://www.elections.virginia.gov/citizen-portal/index.html", "source_name": "Virginia Department of Elections Citizen Portal"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov – How to Register to Vote"}]}
8c5cc113-ad0e-4155-8577-e32c2f061c65	48	TX	Texas	https://teamrv-mvp.sos.texas.gov/MVP/mvp.do	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Texas requires a valid excuse to vote by mail (absentee). Eligible voters include those 65 or older, those with a sickness or disability, those who will be away from their county of residence on Election Day and during the entire early voting period, or those confined in jail but otherwise eligible. Applications for a mail-in ballot must be received by the early voting clerk no later than the 11th day before Election Day (or the 7th day before Election Day if applying in person). Completed ballots must be received by the close of polls on Election Day if mailed, or by 5 p.m. the day after Election Day if the envelope is postmarked by 7 p.m. on Election Day. Ballots may be returned by mail or delivered in person to the early voting clerk's office.	Texas polls are open from 7:00 a.m. to 7:00 p.m. on Election Day statewide. Any voter in line by 7:00 p.m. is entitled to cast a ballot. Early voting hours may vary by county.	Texas requires voters to present an approved photo ID at the polls. Acceptable forms include a Texas Driver License, Texas Personal ID Card, Texas Election Identification Certificate, Texas Handgun License, U.S. Military ID with photo, U.S. Passport, or U.S. Citizenship Certificate with photo. IDs must generally not be expired by more than four years (voters 70 or older may use an expired ID of any age). Voters who do not have an approved photo ID and cannot reasonably obtain one may sign a Reasonable Impediment Declaration and present a supporting document such as a utility bill, bank statement, or voter registration certificate.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS - Find Your Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://teamrv-mvp.sos.texas.gov/MVP/mvp.do", "source_name": "Vote.org"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://teamrv-mvp.sos.texas.gov/MVP/mvp.do", "source_name": "Texas Secretary of State Voter Registration and Polling Place Locator"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org - Absentee Ballot Information"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov - How to Register to Vote"}]}
6354d789-4e33-42f5-bada-68e87435b884	53	WA	Washington	https://www.vote.org/polling-place-locator	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Washington is an all-mail voting state. All registered voters are automatically sent a ballot by mail before each election. Ballots must be postmarked by Election Day (the first Tuesday after the first Monday in November) or returned to an official drop box or voting center by 8:00 PM on Election Day. Voters may also return ballots in person at any county drop box or voting center. There is no separate request process required since ballots are mailed automatically to all active registered voters.	Washington conducts elections primarily by mail, so traditional polling places are limited. In-person voting centers and drop boxes are available and typically open from 7:00 AM to 8:00 PM on Election Day. Hours for early in-person voting at vote centers vary by county. Any voter in line by 8:00 PM on Election Day is entitled to vote.	Washington does not require voters to show photo ID at the polls for mail ballot return or in-person voting at vote centers. First-time voters who registered by mail and did not provide a driver's license number, state ID number, or last four digits of their Social Security number may be asked to provide a copy of an acceptable ID (such as a utility bill, bank statement, government check, paycheck, or other government document showing name and address) when returning their ballot or voting in person.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://voter.votewa.gov/portal2023/login.aspx", "source_name": "VoteWA Voter Portal"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation - Find My Polling Place"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/polling-place-locator", "source_name": "vote.org"}, {"source_url": "https://voter.votewa.gov/portal2023/login.aspx", "source_name": "VoteWA Voter Portal"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org Absentee Ballot"}, {"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov - How to Register to Vote"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov - How to Register to Vote"}]}
90eaba0b-7e27-49ef-9f28-326adf6985d0	55	WI	Wisconsin	https://myvote.wi.gov/en-us/Find-My-Polling-Place	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Wisconsin allows any registered voter to request an absentee ballot without providing a reason. Absentee ballot requests must be received by the municipal clerk no later than 5:00 PM on the Friday before the election. Completed absentee ballots must be received by the municipal clerk by 8:00 PM on Election Day — ballots postmarked by Election Day but received after that deadline are not counted. Voters may return absentee ballots by mail or in person to their municipal clerk's office.	Wisconsin polling places are open from 7:00 AM to 8:00 PM local time on Election Day. Any voter in line by 8:00 PM is entitled to vote.	Wisconsin requires photo ID to vote at the polls. Acceptable forms of ID include a Wisconsin driver's license or state ID card, U.S. passport, military ID, tribal ID, certain student IDs, and other government-issued photo IDs. Voters who do not have an acceptable photo ID may cast a provisional ballot and have until the Friday after the election to provide qualifying ID to their municipal clerk for the ballot to be counted.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://myvote.wi.gov/en-us/Find-My-Polling-Place", "source_name": "MyVote Wisconsin – Find My Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation – Find My Polling Place"}, {"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS – Can I Vote"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://myvote.wi.gov/en-us/Find-My-Polling-Place", "source_name": "MyVote Wisconsin – Find My Polling Place"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org – Absentee Ballot"}, {"source_url": "https://myvote.wi.gov/en-us/FindMyPollingPlace", "source_name": "MyVote Wisconsin"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov – How to Register to Vote"}]}
f0add7df-b194-4c5f-a3e6-1245fbfb9cbb	06	CA	California	https://www.sos.ca.gov/elections/polling-place	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	California automatically mails a ballot to every registered voter for each election. Voters may return their mail ballot by dropping it in an official drop box, mailing it back (must be postmarked on or before Election Day and received by the county elections office within 7 days after Election Day), or delivering it in person to any polling place or vote center in their county on or before Election Day. Voters can also choose to vote in person at a vote center instead of using their mail ballot.	Polling hours in California are statewide: polls open at 7:00 AM and close at 8:00 PM local time on Election Day. Any voter in line by 8:00 PM is entitled to vote.	California does not require voters to show photo ID at the polls in most cases. First-time voters who registered by mail and did not provide a driver's license number, state ID number, or last four digits of their Social Security number during registration may be asked to show identification (such as a utility bill, bank statement, or other document showing name and address). The vast majority of voters are not required to present ID.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.sos.ca.gov/elections/polling-place", "source_name": "California Secretary of State – Find Your Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation – Find My Polling Place"}, {"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov – How to Register to Vote"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.sos.ca.gov/elections/polling-place", "source_name": "California Secretary of State – Find Your Polling Place"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org – Absentee Ballot Information"}, {"source_url": "https://www.sos.ca.gov/elections/polling-place", "source_name": "California Secretary of State – Find Your Polling Place"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov – How to Register to Vote"}]}
0ce0e5b9-60ad-4ce8-80aa-33abc76660e4	24	MD	Maryland	https://elections.maryland.gov/voting/where.html	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Maryland voters may request an absentee (mail-in) ballot without providing a reason. For primary and general elections, mail-in ballot requests must typically be submitted by the Tuesday before Election Day. Completed ballots must be received by election authorities by 8:00 PM on Election Day if hand-delivered, or postmarked by Election Day and received within 10 days after the election if returned by mail. Ballots may be returned by mail, dropped at an official drop box, or delivered in person to a local board of elections.	Maryland polling places are generally open from 7:00 AM to 8:00 PM on Election Day. Any voter in line by 8:00 PM is entitled to cast a ballot.	Maryland does not require voters to show photo ID at the polls in most cases. First-time voters who registered by mail and did not provide verification at registration may be asked to show ID (such as a current utility bill, bank statement, government check, paycheck, or other government document showing name and address). Voters who cannot provide ID may cast a provisional ballot.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://elections.maryland.gov/voting/where.html", "source_name": "Maryland State Board of Elections – Where to Vote"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation – Find My Polling Place"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://elections.maryland.gov/voting/where.html", "source_name": "Maryland State Board of Elections – Where to Vote"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org – Absentee Ballot"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov – How to Register to Vote"}]}
90b1c0fc-35a0-4b62-b771-615cb7aeed7a	26	MI	Michigan	https://mvic.sos.state.mi.us/Voter/Index	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Michigan allows any registered voter to request an absentee ballot without providing a reason. Voters may request an absentee ballot up to 75 days before an election. The completed absentee ballot must be received by the local clerk by 8:00 PM on Election Day to be counted; ballots postmarked by Election Day but received after that deadline are not counted. Voters can return their absentee ballot by mail, in person to their local clerk's office, or by dropping it in an official drop box if one is available in their jurisdiction.	Michigan polling places are open from 7:00 AM to 8:00 PM local time on Election Day. Any voter who is in line by 8:00 PM is entitled to vote.	Michigan does not require voters to show photo ID at the polls as a strict requirement. Voters who do not have an acceptable photo ID may sign an affidavit attesting to their identity and still cast a regular ballot. Acceptable photo IDs include a Michigan driver's license, state ID, passport, military ID, or student ID. Voters who register on Election Day must provide proof of residency.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://mvic.sos.state.mi.us/Voter/Index", "source_name": "Michigan Voter Information Center (SOS)"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation - Find My Polling Place"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://mvic.sos.state.mi.us/Voter/Index", "source_name": "Michigan Voter Information Center (SOS)"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org - Absentee Ballot"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov - How to Register to Vote"}]}
d042493e-94f9-4988-be10-c7310c410693	28	MS	Mississippi	https://www.sos.ms.gov/elections-voting/polling-place-locator	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Mississippi allows absentee voting for qualified voters who meet specific eligibility criteria (e.g., illness, disability, absence from county on Election Day). Absentee ballot applications must be received by the circuit clerk no later than 5 days before the election. Completed ballots must be returned to the circuit clerk by 5:00 PM on the day before the election, or postmarked by Election Day and received within 5 days after the election for certain categories. Ballots may be returned by mail or in person to the circuit clerk's office.	Mississippi polling places are generally open from 7:00 AM to 7:00 PM on Election Day. Voters in line by 7:00 PM are entitled to cast their ballot.	Mississippi requires voters to present an approved photo ID at the polls. Acceptable forms include a Mississippi driver's license, U.S. passport, government employee ID, firearms license, military ID, tribal ID, or a Mississippi Voter ID card (available free from the Secretary of State). Voters who do not have an approved photo ID may cast an affidavit ballot, which will be counted if the voter returns with an approved ID by the Friday following the election or signs a religious objection form.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.sos.ms.gov/elections-voting/polling-place-locator", "source_name": "Mississippi Secretary of State – Polling Place Locator"}, {"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS – Find Your Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.sos.ms.gov/elections-voting/polling-place-locator", "source_name": "Vote.org"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.sos.ms.gov/elections-voting/polling-place-locator", "source_name": "Mississippi Secretary of State – Polling Place Locator"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org – Absentee Ballot"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov – How to Register to Vote"}]}
4d49eccc-e8e0-4a69-bd5c-a2d0ce1d1e2e	31	NE	Nebraska	https://www.votercheck.necvr.ne.gov/VoterView/PollingPlaceSearch.do	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Nebraska voters may vote by mail (early/absentee ballot) without needing an excuse. Voters must request an absentee ballot from their county election office. For primary and general elections, requests must typically be received by the Friday before the election. Completed ballots must be returned to the county election office and received by the close of polls on Election Day, or postmarked by Election Day and received within a specified window. Ballots may be returned by mail or delivered in person to the county election office.	Nebraska polling places are generally open from 8:00 AM to 8:00 PM local time on Election Day. However, hours may vary by county, and voters should confirm with their local county election office.	Nebraska requires voters to show photo ID at the polls. Acceptable forms of photo ID include a Nebraska driver's license or state ID, a U.S. passport, a military ID, or other government-issued photo ID. Voters who do not have an acceptable photo ID may cast a provisional ballot and must provide ID to their county election office by the canvass deadline for the ballot to be counted.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS – Find Your Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.votercheck.necvr.ne.gov/VoterView/PollingPlaceSearch.do", "source_name": "Vote.org"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.votercheck.necvr.ne.gov/VoterView/PollingPlaceSearch.do", "source_name": "Nebraska Voter Check – Polling Place Search"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org – Absentee Ballot"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov – How to Register to Vote"}]}
8512d3be-2408-43e6-ae15-bc9ce8195f47	33	NH	New Hampshire	https://app.sos.nh.gov/statelistclerkandpolling	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	New Hampshire allows absentee voting for qualified voters. Voters must request an absentee ballot from their town or city clerk. Absentee ballot requests must be received by the clerk no later than the day before the election. Completed absentee ballots must be received by the town or city clerk by 5:00 PM on Election Day (not merely postmarked). Ballots may be returned by mail or delivered in person to the clerk's office.	In New Hampshire, polls are generally open from 6:00 AM to 7:00 PM on Election Day statewide, though some smaller towns may open later (as late as 11:00 AM) by local option. Voters in line by closing time are permitted to vote.	New Hampshire requires voters to show photo ID at the polls. Acceptable forms include a New Hampshire driver's license, non-driver ID, passport, military ID, or other government-issued photo ID. First-time voters who registered by mail and have not previously voted in a federal election may also be required to show ID. Voters without an acceptable photo ID may cast a challenged ballot after signing an affidavit and having their photo taken.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS – Find Your Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation – Find My Polling Place"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://app.sos.nh.gov/statelistclerkandpolling", "source_name": "New Hampshire Secretary of State – Clerk and Polling Place List"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org – Absentee Ballot"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov – How to Register to Vote"}]}
c0be4b73-72ca-4888-a87b-8ad66466086e	34	NJ	New Jersey	https://voter.svrs.nj.gov/polling-place-search	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	New Jersey is an all-mail ballot state. All active registered voters are automatically sent a mail-in ballot for each election. Voters who wish to vote by mail must return their ballot by 8:00 PM on Election Day if hand-delivered to a county board of elections or an official drop box, or it must be postmarked by Election Day and received within 7 days after the election if returned by mail. Voters may also request a mail-in ballot online, by mail, or in person through their county clerk up to 7 days before the election, or in person up to the day before the election.	Polls in New Jersey are open statewide from 6:00 AM to 8:00 PM on Election Day. Any voter who is in line by 8:00 PM is entitled to vote.	New Jersey does not require photo ID for most voters at the polls. First-time voters who registered by mail and did not provide sufficient identification during registration may be asked to show ID, which can include a current utility bill, bank statement, government check, paycheck, or other government document showing name and address. Photo ID is not required for established registered voters.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.state.nj.us/state/elections/vote-polling-location", "source_name": "New Jersey Elections – Polling Location"}, {"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS – Find Your Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://voter.svrs.nj.gov/polling-place-search", "source_name": "Vote.org"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://voter.svrs.nj.gov/polling-place-search", "source_name": "New Jersey Polling Place Search (SVRS)"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org – Absentee Ballot Information"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov – How to Register to Vote"}]}
b71e76f2-78a2-4119-8580-ee93477d4dca	36	NY	New York	https://voterlookup.elections.ny.gov	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	New York voters may request an absentee ballot without needing to provide an excuse. Absentee ballot applications must be received by the county board of elections no later than 7 days before the election if submitted by mail, or by the day before the election if submitted in person. Completed absentee ballots must be postmarked by Election Day and received by the board of elections no later than 7 days after the election, or returned in person to the board of elections by the close of polls on Election Day.	In New York, polls are open statewide from 6:00 AM to 9:00 PM on Election Day.	New York does not require photo ID for most voters at the polls. First-time voters who registered by mail and did not provide a valid ID number (driver's license number or last four digits of Social Security number) during registration may be asked to show identification, such as a utility bill, bank statement, government check, paycheck, or other government document showing name and address. Voters who are already on the rolls and have voted before generally do not need to show any ID.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS – Find Your Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/polling-place-locator", "source_name": "vote.org"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://voterlookup.elections.ny.gov", "source_name": "New York State Voter Lookup (NYS Board of Elections)"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org – Absentee Ballot Information"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov – How to Register to Vote"}]}
b929a2c8-47bc-4c66-ad1c-ea92aeba6e87	38	ND	North Dakota	https://vip.sos.nd.gov/wheretovote.aspx	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	North Dakota does not require voter registration. Any eligible voter may request an absentee ballot. Absentee ballot requests must be received by the county auditor no later than 5:00 PM on the day before the election. Completed absentee ballots must be received by the county auditor by 7:00 PM on Election Day; postmarks do not count. Ballots may be returned by mail or delivered in person to the county auditor's office.	Polls in North Dakota are generally open from 7:00 AM to 7:00 PM local time on Election Day. Specific hours may vary slightly by county, so voters should confirm with their local county auditor.	North Dakota requires voters to present an acceptable form of ID at the polls. Acceptable IDs must include the voter's name, residential address, and date of birth. Accepted forms include a North Dakota driver's license, non-driver ID, tribal government-issued ID, or a long-term care certificate. There is no voter registration in North Dakota, so no registration card is required, but photo ID with address is mandatory. Voters without an acceptable ID may use a poll worker attestation in limited circumstances.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS - Find Your Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation - Find My Polling Place"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://vip.sos.nd.gov/wheretovote.aspx", "source_name": "North Dakota Secretary of State - Where to Vote"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org - Absentee Ballot"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov - How to Register to Vote"}]}
b8f5b483-3362-4d2a-bfec-08985bfc5384	41	OR	Oregon	https://sos.oregon.gov/voting/Pages/drop-box-locator.aspx	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Oregon conducts all elections entirely by mail. Every registered voter is automatically mailed a ballot approximately 2 weeks before Election Day. Voters do not need to request a ballot. Completed ballots must be returned by 8:00 PM on Election Day, either by mail (postmarked by Election Day) or dropped off at an official drop box or county elections office by the 8:00 PM closing time on Election Day. No postage is required to return a ballot by mail in Oregon.	Oregon is an all-mail voting state and does not have traditional polling places with set hours. However, official ballot drop boxes and county elections offices are available for in-person ballot return. Drop boxes are generally accessible around the clock in the weeks leading up to Election Day and close at 8:00 PM on Election Day. Voters needing in-person assistance should contact their county elections office for specific hours.	Oregon does not require voters to show photo ID at a polling place because the state votes entirely by mail. First-time voters who registered by mail and did not provide a driver's license number, state ID number, or last four digits of their Social Security number may be required to provide a copy of an acceptable ID document with their ballot. Acceptable documents include a utility bill, bank statement, government check, paycheck, or other government document showing the voter's name and address.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://sos.oregon.gov/voting/Pages/drop-box-locator.aspx", "source_name": "Oregon Secretary of State – Drop Box Locator"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS – Find Your Polling Place"}, {"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov – How to Register to Vote"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://sos.oregon.gov/voting/Pages/drop-box-locator.aspx", "source_name": "Oregon Secretary of State – Drop Box Locator"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://sos.oregon.gov/voting/Pages/drop-box-locator.aspx", "source_name": "Oregon Secretary of State – Drop Box Locator"}, {"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov – How to Register to Vote"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov – How to Register to Vote"}]}
50d14262-e15f-4f96-a1fc-9ff179169348	45	SC	South Carolina	https://vrems.scvotes.sc.gov/Voter/Login?PageMode=PollingPlace	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	South Carolina voters may vote absentee (by mail) without needing to provide a reason. Absentee ballot applications must be received by the county voter registration office no later than 5 days before the election. Completed absentee ballots must be returned by mail or in person and received by the county board by 7:00 PM on Election Day; postmarks do not count. Voters may also return their absentee ballot in person to their county election office before Election Day.	In South Carolina, polls are generally open from 7:00 AM to 7:00 PM on Election Day statewide. Any voter in line by 7:00 PM is entitled to cast a ballot.	South Carolina requires photo ID to vote in person. Acceptable forms of photo ID include a South Carolina driver's license, SC DMV ID card, SC voter registration card with photo, federal military ID, or US passport. Voters who do not have an acceptable photo ID may cast a provisional ballot and must return to the county election office by the next business day after the election to show ID or sign an affidavit claiming a reasonable impediment to obtaining ID.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS - Find Your Polling Place"}, {"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation - Find My Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://vrems.scvotes.sc.gov/Voter/Login?PageMode=PollingPlace", "source_name": "Vote.org"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://vrems.scvotes.sc.gov/Voter/Login?PageMode=PollingPlace", "source_name": "South Carolina State Election Commission Polling Place Locator"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org - Absentee Ballot Information"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov - How to Register to Vote"}]}
67fba3bf-dbb7-4372-bac5-c2bad1e18c43	54	WV	West Virginia	https://apps.sos.wv.gov/Elections/voter/FindMyPollingPlace	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	West Virginia allows any registered voter to vote absentee by mail without needing an excuse. Voters must request an absentee ballot from their county clerk. For primary and general elections, absentee ballot applications must typically be received by the county clerk no later than 6 days before the election. Completed ballots must be received by the county clerk by 7:30 PM on Election Day; postmarks alone are not sufficient — the ballot must be physically received by the deadline. Ballots may be returned by mail or delivered in person to the county clerk's office.	West Virginia polling places are generally open from 6:30 AM to 7:30 PM local time on Election Day. Any voter in line by 7:30 PM is entitled to cast a ballot. Specific hours may vary slightly by county, so voters should confirm with their local county clerk.	West Virginia requires voters to show ID at the polls. Acceptable forms of photo ID include a WV driver's license, U.S. passport, military ID, or other government-issued photo ID. Voters who do not have an acceptable photo ID may cast a provisional ballot and must provide ID to the county clerk within two days after the election for the ballot to be counted. First-time voters who registered by mail and did not provide ID verification during registration may also be subject to additional ID requirements.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://apps.sos.wv.gov/Elections/voter/FindMyPollingPlace", "source_name": "West Virginia Secretary of State – Find My Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation – Find My Polling Place"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://apps.sos.wv.gov/Elections/voter/FindMyPollingPlace", "source_name": "West Virginia Secretary of State – Find My Polling Place"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org – Absentee Ballot"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov – How to Register to Vote"}]}
5aeda026-e380-4cba-bdb4-8108521abb77	08	CO	Colorado	https://www.coloradosos.gov/voter/pages/pub/olvr/findVoterReg.xhtml	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Colorado is an all-mail voting state. All active registered voters are automatically sent a mail ballot before each election. Voters may return their completed ballot by mail (must be postmarked by Election Day and received within 8 days after Election Day), dropped off at an official drop box or voter service and polling center by 7:00 PM on Election Day, or delivered in person to a voter service and polling center. Voters may also choose to vote in person at a voter service and polling center during the early voting period or on Election Day.	Colorado voter service and polling centers are open from 7:00 AM to 7:00 PM on Election Day. Early in-person voting at voter service and polling centers typically begins 15 days before Election Day. Specific hours for early voting locations may vary by county.	Colorado does not require voters to show photo ID at the polls in the traditional sense. First-time voters who registered by mail and did not provide ID verification during registration may be asked to provide identification. Accepted forms include a Colorado driver's license number, state ID number, or the last four digits of a Social Security number provided at registration. Voters who cannot verify identity may cast a provisional ballot.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS - Find Your Polling Place"}, {"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation - Find My Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.coloradosos.gov/voter/pages/pub/olvr/findVoterReg.xhtml", "source_name": "Vote.org"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.coloradosos.gov/voter/pages/pub/olvr/findVoterReg.xhtml", "source_name": "Colorado Secretary of State Voter Registration/Polling Place Lookup"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org - Absentee Ballot Information"}, {"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov - How to Register to Vote"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov - How to Register to Vote"}]}
902b1b40-c2ae-4806-9abe-7dc892cb09c4	12	FL	Florida	https://dos.myflorida.com/elections/for-voters/check-your-voter-status-and-polling-place/voter-precinct-lookup	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Florida voters may request a vote-by-mail ballot without providing a reason. Requests must be received by the Supervisor of Elections no later than 7 days before the election. Completed ballots must be received by the Supervisor of Elections by 7:00 PM on Election Day; postmarks do not count. Ballots may be returned by mail, in person to the Supervisor of Elections office, or dropped off at any early voting site or Election Day polling place in the voter's county.	In Florida, polls are open statewide from 7:00 AM to 7:00 PM on Election Day. Any voter in line by 7:00 PM is entitled to cast a ballot.	Florida requires voters to present photo and signature ID at the polls. Acceptable forms include a Florida driver's license, Florida ID card, U.S. passport, military ID, or other government-issued photo ID with signature. Voters who do not have an acceptable photo ID may cast a provisional ballot, which will be counted if the signature on the ballot envelope matches the signature on file with the Supervisor of Elections.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS – Find Your Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://dos.myflorida.com/elections/for-voters/check-your-voter-status-and-polling-place/voter-precinct-lookup", "source_name": "Vote.org"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://dos.myflorida.com/elections/for-voters/check-your-voter-status-and-polling-place/voter-precinct-lookup", "source_name": "Florida Division of Elections – Voter Precinct Lookup"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org – Absentee Ballot"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov – How to Register to Vote"}]}
3bed74ba-d51f-40ea-95ee-57293bc78b03	15	HI	Hawaii	https://elections.hawaii.gov/resources/county-election-divisions	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Hawaii conducts elections entirely by mail. All registered voters are automatically mailed a ballot before each election. Voters may return their ballot by mail (must be postmarked by Election Day and received within 7 days after Election Day), deposited in an official drop box by 7:00 PM on Election Day, or returned in person at a voter service center by the close of polls on Election Day.	Hawaii does not have traditional polling places; it is an all-mail voting state. However, voter service centers are open for in-person voting and ballot drop-off. Voter service centers are generally open from 8:00 AM to 4:00 PM on weekdays during the early voting period, and from 7:00 AM to 7:00 PM on Election Day. Specific hours may vary by county.	Hawaii does not require voters to show photo ID at the polls in most circumstances. Voters who are voting for the first time and registered by mail may be asked to provide identification. Otherwise, poll workers verify voter identity using the state voter registration database. No ID is required for the vast majority of Hawaii voters.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://elections.hawaii.gov/resources/county-election-divisions", "source_name": "Hawaii Office of Elections – County Election Divisions"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS – Can I Vote: Find Your Polling Place"}, {"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation – Find My Polling Place"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://elections.hawaii.gov/resources/county-election-divisions", "source_name": "Hawaii Office of Elections – County Election Divisions"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org – Absentee Ballot Information"}, {"source_url": "https://elections.hawaii.gov/resources/county-election-divisions", "source_name": "Hawaii Office of Elections – County Election Divisions"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov – How to Register to Vote"}]}
5a937a11-a561-4951-83e2-853e5492de17	16	ID	Idaho	https://elections.sos.idaho.gov/ElectionLink/ElectionLink/ViewPollingLocation.aspx	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Idaho allows any registered voter to request an absentee ballot without providing a reason. Absentee ballot requests must be received by the county clerk no later than 11 days before the election. Completed ballots must be received by the county clerk by 8:00 PM on Election Day; ballots postmarked by Election Day but received after that deadline are not counted. Voters may return their absentee ballot by mail or by delivering it in person to the county clerk's office.	In Idaho, polls are generally open from 8:00 AM to 8:00 PM local time on Election Day. Hours are consistent statewide, though voters should confirm with their local county clerk as specific locations may vary.	Idaho requires voters to show photo ID at the polls. Acceptable forms include a current Idaho driver's license, state-issued ID card, U.S. passport, tribal ID, or other government-issued photo ID. Voters who do not have a qualifying photo ID may sign a Personal Identification Affidavit at the polling place in order to cast a ballot.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS – Find Your Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://elections.sos.idaho.gov/ElectionLink/ElectionLink/ViewPollingLocation.aspx", "source_name": "Vote.org"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://elections.sos.idaho.gov/ElectionLink/ElectionLink/ViewPollingLocation.aspx", "source_name": "Idaho Secretary of State – Polling Place Locator"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org – Absentee Ballot"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov – How to Register to Vote"}]}
a5cb6dce-3afd-42fb-af3c-7ddb6caa5233	19	IA	Iowa	https://sos.iowa.gov/elections/voterreg/pollingplace/search.aspx	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Iowa voters may request an absentee ballot without providing a reason. Absentee ballot requests must be received by the county auditor no later than 15 days before the election. Completed absentee ballots must be received by the county auditor by the time polls close on Election Day (8:00 PM); ballots postmarked by Election Day but received after that deadline are not counted. Voters may return absentee ballots by mail or by delivering them in person to the county auditor's office.	Iowa polling places are open from 7:00 AM to 8:00 PM local time on Election Day statewide.	Iowa requires voters to show photo ID at the polls. Acceptable forms of ID include an Iowa driver's license or non-operator ID, U.S. passport, U.S. military ID, Iowa voter ID card, or tribal ID with photo. Voters who do not have an acceptable photo ID may cast a provisional ballot and must provide ID or have their identity confirmed by an election official within the provisional ballot deadline for their vote to count. Iowa issues a free voter ID card to registered voters who lack other qualifying ID.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS – Find Your Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation – Voter ID Laws"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://sos.iowa.gov/elections/voterreg/pollingplace/search.aspx", "source_name": "Iowa Secretary of State – Polling Place Search"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org – Absentee Ballot"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov – How to Register to Vote"}]}
36f61340-57f0-4a97-b05b-9631cb44e744	21	KY	Kentucky	https://vrsws.sos.ky.gov/vic	https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote	Kentucky allows any registered voter to vote absentee (by mail) without needing an excuse. Voters must request an absentee ballot from their county clerk. For regular elections, absentee ballot requests must be submitted at least 7 days before Election Day. Completed ballots must be received by the county clerk's office by 6:00 PM on Election Day, or postmarked by Election Day and received within 5 days after the election. Ballots may be returned by mail or delivered in person to the county clerk's office.	Kentucky polling places are generally open from 6:00 AM to 6:00 PM local time on Election Day. Hours are uniform statewide under state law.	Kentucky requires voters to present photo ID at the polls. Acceptable forms include a Kentucky driver's license or state ID, U.S. passport, military ID, or other government-issued photo ID. Voters who do not have a photo ID may cast a provisional ballot and must return to the county clerk's office by the close of business on the sixth day after the election to show valid ID for the ballot to be counted. Kentucky offers free state photo IDs for voting purposes.	{"polling_hours": [{"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS Can I Vote – Find Your Polling Place"}, {"source_url": "https://www.usvotefoundation.org/find-my-polling-place", "source_name": "U.S. Vote Foundation – Find My Polling Place"}], "id_requirements": [{"source_url": "https://vrsws.sos.ky.gov/vic", "source_name": "Vote.org"}], "polling_place_url": [{"source_url": "https://vrsws.sos.ky.gov/vic", "source_name": "Kentucky Voter Information Center (SOS)"}, {"source_url": "https://www.nass.org/can-i-vote/find-your-polling-place", "source_name": "NASS Can I Vote – Find Your Polling Place"}], "vote_by_mail_info": [{"source_url": "https://www.vote.org/absentee-ballot", "source_name": "vote.org – Absentee Ballot"}], "voter_registration_url": [{"source_url": "https://www.usa.gov/register-to-vote", "source_name": "USAGov – How to Register to Vote"}]}
